\pard\nowidctlpar\nooverflow\qc\sl240\slmult1{\b\ul\f0\lang1033\fs20\kerning28 THE
 TRANSMITTER CIRCUIT\par}

\pard\nowidctlpar\nooverflow\sl240\slmult1{\f0\lang1033\fs20\kerning28 The really
 neat thing about these transducers is the fact that they are designed to transmit
 and receive.  If you have two ultrasonic transducers, you can send and receive.  
Of course you\u8217\'92ll need two breadboards or two PCBs.  Let\u8217\'92s talk
 about the transmitter, shall we?  I have personally tested these transducers are
 frequencies as low as 20kHz, to as high as 50kHz.  You can still transmit and receive
 information at these frequencies, despite the fact that the center frequency is
 40kHz.\par}

\pard\nowidctlpar\nooverflow\sl240\slmult1{\f0\lang1033\fs20\kerning28\par}

\pard\nowidctlpar\nooverflow\sl240\slmult1{\f0\lang1033\fs20\kerning28 On the following
 page, you\u8217\'92ll see a diagram for the transmitter circuit.  I\u8217\'92ve
 offered a part list, but there are many passive component values (resistors/capacitors
/transistors)  that can be modified to achieve maximum output power.  The part list
 I am offering is a \u8220\'93safe\u8221\'94 list of components to use.  By this
, I mean I\u8217\'92ve used these components to go in conjunction with my video 
advertisement. That\u8217\'92s right!  The components I\u8217\'92ve listed here, 
are the components you saw in my video.  Note that this not my most efficient circuit
 for transmitting/receiving, but it is good for beginners, and those who are simply
 a little rusty.  The receiver circuit can be modified greatly as well.  Anyhow, 
back to the transmitter.\par}

\pard\nowidctlpar\nooverflow\sl240\slmult1{\f0\lang1033\fs20\kerning28\par}

\pard\nowidctlpar\nooverflow\sl240\slmult1{\f0\lang1033\fs20\kerning28 Perhaps you
\u8217\'92ve noticed by looking at the diagram that the transmitter circuit is fairly
 simple.  We really only have a few blocks of information to discuss. Let\u8217\'92
s start with out 555 timer oscillator.  For those of you who are new to the 555 
timer, we\u8217\'92ve got our 555 timer configured in astable mode; an oscillator
.  By this, it is meant that we will be using this chip to create our transmission
 frequency.  If you google search \u8220\'93555 timer calculator\u8221\'94 you can
 determine which components you\u8217\'92re going to need to create certain frequencies
.  The components that determine the frequency and duty cycle of the 555 timer in
 astable mode are the following: \par}

\pard\nowidctlpar\nooverflow\sl240\slmult1{\f0\lang1033\fs20\kerning28 R5 = RA;  
R6 = RB;  C1 = C\par}

\pard\nowidctlpar\nooverflow\sl240\slmult1{\f0\lang1033\fs20\kerning28 Now, you 
have the option of placing a 0.01uF tantalum or ceramic capacitor between pin#5 
and the ground line, but it is not necessary.  This would be used in higher frequency
 applications.  You may get a slightly better resolution if you implement this capacitor
, but I did not use it, so I will not be adding this component to the parts list
.\par}

\pard\nowidctlpar\nooverflow\sl240\slmult1{\f0\lang1033\fs20\kerning28     \par}

